---

// Editorial page: fully static, rendered at build time.
export const prerender = true;
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { OFFICIAL_HOSTS } from '../data/games';
---

<BaseLayout>
  <header class="page-head container--wide">
    <span class="eyebrow">Walkthrough</span>
    <h1>Parking Fury Walkthrough: How the 10 Levels Progress</h1>
    <p>
      A walkthrough is only worth reading if it is true. This page separates what the official
      sources confirm about Parking Fury’s level structure from general method that transfers
      across every layout — and it includes fully tested, step-by-step walkthroughs for the
      three-lot mini-game we built ourselves.
    </p>
    <div class="chip-row">
      <span class="pill">10 levels confirmed</span>
      <span class="pill">Sourced to Coolmath Games</span>
      <span class="pill">Mini-game steps tested</span>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section container prose">
    <div class="note note--plain">
      <span aria-hidden="true">✓</span>
      <span>
        <strong>Our walkthrough policy:</strong> we only publish level notes we can back with a
        source or with our own tested play. Step-by-step directions for individual Parking Fury
        levels are not on this page yet — we will not invent them. Everything below is either
        attributed, obviously general advice, or written about a game we can verify end to end.
      </span>
    </div>

    <h2>What is confirmed about the original’s 10 levels</h2>
    <p>
      Coolmath Games — the official host — confirms the original Parking Fury has
      <strong>10 levels</strong>, and that they do not stay gentle: their FAQ flags levels 8 and 9
      as the point where most players feel the difficulty spike. They also publish timing
      expectations that are useful for calibrating your own progress:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>First playthrough:</strong> roughly 20–30 minutes for a beginner.</li>
      <li><strong>With practice:</strong> getting the run under 10 minutes is described as realistic.</li>
      <li><strong>Recorded fastest run:</strong> about 7½ minutes, according to the official speedrun site cited in Coolmath’s FAQ.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The sequels, Parking Fury 2 and 3, are confirmed to reuse the same handling with new maps —
      but <a href="/parking-fury-2/">no level count is published for them</a>, so this page does
      not quote one. Source:
      <a href="https://www.coolmathgames.com/0-parking-fury" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Parking Fury’s official page on Coolmath Games<span class="sr-only">(opens in a new tab)</span></a>.
    </p>

    <h2>The five-phase method that works on every layout</h2>
    <p>
      You cannot script moves you have not seen, but parking layouts reward a fixed routine.
      Run this sequence on every level and the failures move from “I crashed” to “I misjudged the
      angle” — which is much easier to correct:
    </p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li><strong>Read the bay before you move.</strong> Find the yellow rectangle, note which side is open, and look for the tightest pinch point on the way in — that pinch, not the bay, is usually what ends the run.</li>
      <li><strong>Straighten your approach.</strong> Use the open road to line the car up with the bay’s entry direction while you still have space. The last correction should be tiny, not a U-turn.</li>
      <li><strong>Brake in the straight, coast in the turn.</strong> Do all your slowing before the steering starts; entering the final metre at walking pace leaves room for one more correction.</li>
      <li><strong>Steer in taps.</strong> Short presses nudge the nose without swinging the tail into a neighbour. On touch, small wheel rotations do the same job.</li>
      <li><strong>Stop fully inside the lines.</strong> A car half-in still counts as an attempt, not a park. Once you clip something, restart the attempt — recovering from a scrape usually costs more than a clean retry.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Recurring situations and how to handle them</h2>
    <h3>Nose-in bays along a wall</h3>
    <p>
      The most common shape: a marked rectangle with neighbours on one or both sides. Approach
      perpendicular to the wall, not diagonally — a diagonal entry saves no space and costs you
      the tail swing. Keep the car’s nose aimed at the far corner of the bay and let forward
      motion walk it in.
    </p>
    <h3>Bays with an obstacle mid-path</h3>
    <p>
      When a parked car or wall juts into the road, treat it as a lane change, not an obstacle
      course: move early and wide while you are far away from the bay, then return to your line.
      Swerving late — close to the bay — is what swings the tail into things.
    </p>
    <h3>After a scrape</h3>
    <p>
      Official descriptions warn that crashing forces a retry, so do not try to reverse out of a
      pinched position and salvage it. Reset, and spend the saved time on the clean approach you
      already know how to do.
    </p>

    <h2>Walkthrough: our original mini-game, all 3 lots</h2>
    <p>
      The mini-game on <a href="/games/tight-lot/">its own game page</a> was written for this site, so these
      steps are exact — we can vouch for every coordinate. Three lots, escalating difficulty:
    </p>

    <h3>Lot 1 — Straight-in bay</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>You start at the bottom of the lot facing the back wall; the gold dashed bay sits top-centre, flanked by two parked cars.</li>
      <li>Drive straight up the centre lane — two parked cars sit mid-lot, one left and one right, and the middle is clear.</li>
      <li>Lift off around the halfway line, then tap steering to line the nose up with the gap between the flanking cars.</li>
      <li>Creep into the bay and stop once the whole car is inside the gold dashes. The game wants the car nearly straight and below walking speed before it counts the park.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Lot 2 — Parallel park</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>You start on the right side of the road, facing left. The open bay is in the top row, between two groups of parked cars.</li>
      <li>Hold left and drive past the gap — a lone car is parked near the middle of the road, so swing around it early rather than at the last moment.</li>
      <li>Stop well past the gap (roughly level with the left-hand parked cars), then hold reverse and tap steering to swing the rear toward the bay.</li>
      <li>Alternate reverse and small steer taps until the car sits square in the gap, then nudge forward or back to centre it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Lot 3 — The reverse lane</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>The lot is a corridor around a large central block. From the start, keep to the gap between the left wall and the block — the left wall juts outward, so hugging far left will clip it.</li>
      <li>Climb to the top corridor and turn right. Take the corner wide: the inside of the turn is the wall, and long turns clip it.</li>
      <li>Follow the top lane toward the right corner. A parked car sits in the right-hand lane below the bay — pass to its right.</li>
      <li>Straighten up and nose into the vertical bay in the top-right corner. Stop fully inside the dashes.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="btn-row">
      <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/games/tight-lot/">Play the mini-game</a>
      <a class="btn btn--secondary" href="/how-to-play/">Mini-game controls</a>
    </div>

    <h2>Practising the real thing</h2>
    <p>
      The mini-game builds the routine; the official games test it with their own layouts. The
      licensed copies are one click away, and this site never blocks them behind anything:
    </p>
    <div class="btn-row">
      <a class="btn btn--primary" href={OFFICIAL_HOSTS.coolmath} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        Play Parking Fury on Coolmath Games<span class="sr-only">(opens in a new tab)</span>
      </a>
      <a class="btn btn--secondary" href={OFFICIAL_HOSTS.coolmathSeries} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        All three originals<span class="sr-only">(opens in a new tab)</span>
      </a>
    </div>

    <h2>When a level stalls you</h2>
    <p>
      Most “impossible level” moments are one of three things: too much speed on entry, an
      approach angle too tight to finish, or a recovery attempt after a scrape. The
      <a href="/tips/">tips and troubleshooting page</a> works through those in order, and the
      <a href="/how-to-play/">controls page</a> covers input quirks such as steering that seems to
      do nothing while stationary.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
